Start here
==========

This is a Windows port of litmus (https://github.com/notroj/litmus).
If you are picking this up cold, read these in order:

  1. HANDOFF.md   where the current work stopped and what is next
  2. README.md    what this fork is, how to build it, how to run it
  3. AGENTS.md    how to use litmus to test a WebDAV server, and how to
                  read the results; also the JSON schema
  4. PATCHES.md   what was changed inside neon/ and why, needed before
                  you update the bundled neon
  5. This file    what is left to do

Layout:

  src/            the test suites, one .c per suite, plus common.c,
                  the suite registry (suites.h, litmus-cli.c) and the
                  benchmark (bench.c)
  neon/           vendored neon 0.37.1, checked in, not a submodule
  neon/test/common/tests.c
                  the shared harness: result formatting, counters,
                  JSON output, run_suite(). Patched; see PATCHES.md
  win32/config.h  generated config.h, checked in so Makefile.w32 works
                  without autotools
  VERSION         the fork version, read by both build paths
  tests/          test harnesses and expected results; see below
  Makefile.w32    the no-autotools build
  .github/        CI: both build paths, self-test, release

Everything is one executable, litmus-cli, with the suite as a
subcommand:

  litmus-cli basic http://host/dav/
  litmus-cli locks --json http://host/dav/
  litmus-cli all http://host/dav/
  litmus-cli bench http://host/dav/
  litmus-cli list

Test harnesses in tests/:

  wsgidav.sh      runs the suites against a local wsgidav; --check
                  compares against expected-wsgidav.txt.  This is the
                  gate: it must print "All suites match the expected
                  results" before and after every change.
  godav.sh        runs them against golang.org/x/net/webdav instead.
                  Needed because wsgidav cannot exercise the lock
                  tests at all (see below).
  capture.sh      captures every suite in every output mode, for the
                  byte-for-byte regression check
  compare-captures.sh
                  diffs two capture directories
  hyperfine.sh    times litmus itself rather than the server
  httpd.sh        upstream's Apache-in-a-container harness, Unix only

Neither test server is complete, and the difference matters:

  * wsgidav 4.3.5 supports dead properties, so the props suite is
    meaningful against it, but it answers LOCK with the Content-Type
    "application; charset=utf-8", which is not a media type and not an
    XML one.  neon discards the body unparsed, litmus never sees the
    lock token, and everything downstream of holding a lock is
    skipped.  Any change to the lock tests is invisible there.
  * x/net/webdav locks correctly, including the RFC 4918 section 7.3
    treatment of a LOCK on an unmapped URL, but has no dead property
    store, so four props tests fail against it.

So: verify lock work against godav.sh, property work against
wsgidav.sh, and do not read either result as the whole picture.

Before you change anything
==========================

Run the baseline and note the numbers:

  ./tests/wsgidav.sh --check

It must print "All suites match the expected results". If it does not,
fix that before doing anything else. Everything below assumes a green
baseline.

Conventions, in code and in prose:

  * Binary units, always: KiB, MiB, GiB, powers of 1024. Never KB, MB
    or GB for a quantity of bytes, and never a decimal power of ten.
    Rates are MiB/s.
  * Timestamps are ISO 8601 in UTC with millisecond precision and a
    trailing Z, e.g. 2026-08-18T13:04:54.429Z. Truncate sub-second
    digits, do not round, so a stamp never names a moment that had not
    happened yet.
  * Durations are seconds to millisecond resolution, and are
    wall-clock. Say so when reporting them, because they include server
    and network time.
  * Quote exact byte counts where the exact figure matters, with the
    rounded binary figure in brackets after it.

If you touch neon/test/common/tests.c or src/common.c, the default text
output must stay byte for byte identical. Prove it:

  make -f Makefile.w32 && ./tests/capture.sh /tmp/before
  ... make the change ...
  make -f Makefile.w32 && ./tests/capture.sh /tmp/after
  ./tests/compare-captures.sh /tmp/before /tmp/after

Two captures of the same build are byte for byte identical, so any
difference is the change. Do not skip this. It is the only thing
standing between this fork and silently breaking every script that
parses litmus output.

High value work
===============

1. Docs are stale and must be rewritten
---------------------------------------

README.md, AGENTS.md, PATCHES.md and NEWS still describe nine
executables and do not mention litmus-cli, the bench subcommand, the
new tests or the new options. This is the largest outstanding item and
the one a user notices first.

README.md is to be rewritten objectively and technically: keep what a
user needs to build, install and run it, and cut the narrative, the
history and the commentary about the fork's origins. It should say
plainly that this suite covers more than the version it was forked
from, without explaining why upstream does not have the extra tests and
without editorialising about upstream at all.

Download instructions must use one fixed URL that always points at the
latest release, with no GitHub API calls, no jq and no grep pipelines:
one curl, or one Invoke-WebRequest. That depends on item 2.

2. CI and releases
------------------

* Add .github/dependabot.yml covering the actions.
* Add a concurrency group so a newer run cancels the superseded one
  when several commits land in quick succession.
* Publish the executable individually as a release asset as well as
  inside the zip, with an explicit tag_name and autogenerated notes.
  Asset names must contain no version string, so a fixed "latest" URL
  works. softprops/action-gh-release@v2 is fine for this; do not add
  third-party actions where the gh CLI is just as good.
* Releasing must fail loudly rather than overwrite an existing good
  release.
* The workflow still builds and uploads nine .exe files by name in
  places; it needs to follow the single executable.
* Gate the build on a lint job.
* CI rebuilds the wsgidav virtualenv on every run. Caching it with
  actions/cache would save about half a minute per job.

3. Stable error classification in the JSON
------------------------------------------

`context` is a human-readable string today, so a consumer wanting to
branch on the kind of failure has to pattern-match prose. Add a stable
machine field alongside it: the operation that failed (PUT, GET, MKCOL,
MOVE, DELETE, PROPFIND, LOCK), the HTTP status observed, and the path.
Define the set of kinds once and treat it as part of the JSON contract.
Keep `context` exactly as it is; this is an addition, not a
replacement.

4. Tests for the harness itself
-------------------------------

There are no unit tests. Every check requires a live server, which is
why the wsgidav and godav harnesses exist. The result accounting, the
JSON emission, the escaping, the notrun bookkeeping and the error paths
can all be tested without a network by substituting a fake transport
that returns canned responses. This is the largest testability gap in
the tree and it is what makes changes to tests.c risky enough to need
the capture-and-diff ritual above.

A synthetic suite exercising warnings, contexts and every status was
used while building the JSON output and worked well. Make that a
permanent, checked-in test rather than a throwaway.

5. Optional chunked-upload suite
--------------------------------

Several widely deployed DAV servers implement a chunked upload
extension: MKCOL a per-transfer directory, PUT the parts into it under
zero-padded numeric names, then MOVE a virtual assembly path onto the
final destination. It is not in RFC 4918, so it must be opt-in and must
not affect the default suite list or the expected results, but it is
easy to implement wrongly and there is no convenient conformance test
for it.

Outstanding work
================

Smaller maintenance items, in rough priority order.

* The autotools build's remaining EXEEXT problem is fixed and the
  relink-every-run problem with it, but only for the one executable
  that is left. If another program is ever added, put $(EXEEXT)
  through its rules too and keep `subdirs' an order-only prerequisite.

* Test failure paths leak. Most tests build URLs with ne_concat and
  return through the ON* macros, which do not free anything. The cases
  that left files on disk have been fixed, and lockbomb's threadfn()
  now always destroys its session and hands back a message the caller
  frees. The remaining leaks are memory only, in a process that is
  about to exit, so they are untidy rather than harmful. Cleaning them
  up properly means a cleanup path per test.

* --trace does not cover the lockbomb worker threads or the benchmark
  transfers. They create their own sessions rather than using
  i_session, so the trace hooks are never attached. This is deliberate:
  writing to one FILE from twenty threads would need locking, tracing
  400,000 lock/unlock round trips would not be useful, and dumping the
  benchmark's bodies would dominate the measurement it is trying to
  make. Say so if anyone asks.

* neon 0.37.1 speaks HTTP/1.1 only, so the "pin the protocol version"
  escape hatch that a benchmark would want against servers which are
  worse over HTTP/2 cannot be implemented here. It would need a
  different HTTP client. Worth stating in the docs rather than leaving
  as an open question.

* Nothing reports which suites a server actually supports before
  running them, so `litmus-cli all' against a class 1 server spends its
  time skipping. Not important.

Things that were looked at and deliberately not done
====================================================

* Merging the tolsen fork's tests wholesale, requested in upstream
  issue 8. It is 1643 lines with no shared history, stale by many
  commits, and unreviewed. Individual test cases were lifted from it
  after being read and verified on their own merits: the escaped-space
  collection test, the second-MKCOL check, and the MKCOL 201 and DELETE
  204 status warnings. The rest was either unsound (a MKCOL of a URI
  with a literal space, which is not a valid request target) or
  dependent on a particular server's authentication setup.

* Upstream issue 21 asks for PROPPATCH tests to accept a 207 without
  looking inside it, on the grounds that arbitrary dead properties need
  not be allowed. The first half of that is now implemented properly:
  litmus_proppatch() reports the status from inside the multistatus, so
  a server that refuses with 207+423 is judged on the 423. The second
  half, being lenient about servers with no dead property support at
  all, was rejected upstream with sound reasoning and is not
  implemented here either: the property tests would be pointless.

* Upstream issue 22 (fail_cond_put_unlocked) was analysed upstream and
  litmus's behaviour is correct. Nothing to do.

* Connection reuse was measured, in case it was a performance problem.
  It is not: a props run makes 36 requests over one TCP connection. The
  slowest tests are copy_coll and move_coll in copymove, at about two
  seconds each, and that time is the server copying a collection, not
  litmus. There is nothing to win on the client side.

* Prefixing message bodies in the --trace output the way request and
  response headers are prefixed. Bodies are dumped by neon inside
  square brackets instead. That is better, not worse: PROPFIND and LOCK
  bodies are XML, so nearly every line starts with "<", which is also
  the response-header prefix. The brackets keep the boundary
  unambiguous.
